    {
      "chapter": "lifts",
      "status": "source checked",
      "id": "HVO-01",
      "anchor": "lifts-hermitian-orbit-mean",
      "title": "The nonzero isotropic orbit has mean O(r^(2d(M−1)))",
      "statement": "For one vector in a nondegenerate M-dimensional Hermitian space, M≥3, the mean of the nonzero rational vectors of norm zero meeting B_N(r) is ≤C_N r^(2d(M−1)) for every r>0, with d=[F:ℚ]. The singleton zero contributes exactly the indicator of zero after division by the fundamental domain volume.",
      "hypotheses": "CM E/F, a fixed F-basis of E, N a positive integer, finite coordinate lattices and the sum of absolute real coordinates defining the radius. The mean uses quotient Haar probability. In the split case the hyperbolic complement of dimension s=M−2 has finite arithmetic quotient volume. The shell comparison uses compact representatives transverse to the stabilizer, the parabolic Haar multiplier, and scalar lattice packing in dimension 2d.",
      "correspondence": "The proof first estimates the raw count integral after transferring to the hyperbolic coordinates and dilating a fixed compact set by the inverse radius. The geometric tail starts at ceil(x₀−log r), with ratio exp(−2ds). The compact family of paired vectors has Hermitian pairing one. The comparison does not determine the leading constant or an asymptotic.",
      "dependencies": [
        "ADB-03",
        "PSM-03",
        "UQC-03"
      ],
      "equations": [
        "lifts-hermitian-orbit-average",
        "lifts-hermitian-orbit-shell"
      ],
