    {
      "chapter": "lifts",
      "status": "source checked",
      "id": "HVO-02",
      "anchor": "lifts-hermitian-orbit-compact",
      "title": "Each regular scalar orbit has finite mean on a compact set",
      "statement": "For each fixed t∈F× and compact adelic Ω, the integral of the number of rational vectors of norm t in hΩ is finite. The rational norms that can meet Ω form a finite set. In dimension M≥3 the additional nonzero isotropic orbit is integrable on Ω by the ball estimate, so the mean of all nonzero vectors on Ω is finite.",
      "hypotheses": "CM E/F and nondegenerate Hermitian form. For a represented nonzero t the orthogonal complement of a vector of norm t has finite arithmetic quotient volume (the general theorem takes this as an induction hypothesis in lower dimensions). The compact representative input applies to tuples of nondegenerate Gram matrix, here the single scalar t. The ambient Haar fundamental domain has finite positive volume for normalization.",
      "correspondence": "Witt extension gives transitivity over the rational points and, adelically, closed-orbit transitivity. Compact lifting supplies representatives C above the compact part of the regular orbit. For f(h)=1_Ω(h⁻¹e) its support lies in G₁C⁻¹. The compact-cover bound compares the unfolded integral with the finite stabilizer volume. Only when assembling the whole compact mean is the nonzero isotropic estimate invoked.",
      "dependencies": [
        "HVO-01",
        "RGL-01",
        "AGF-03",
        "UQC-03"
      ],
